  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2-x)$ at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2-x) \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(1)=-\\frac{1}{(2-1)^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
